"A logical network diagram shows addressing domains, routing relationships, gateways, and relevant overlay connectivity.\n\nUse when: Review subnet design and traffic routes. Explain hybrid or hub-and-spoke network connectivity.\n\nAvoid when: Exact cable endpoints or application message order is required.\n\nVisual grammar: Padded regions encode networks, subnets, or routing domains. Gateway and router nodes show path selection boundaries. Links identify peering, tunnels, or routed connectivity with concise route annotations.\n\nBuild: Choose address families, environment, and routing scope. Place subnets with verified or proposed address ranges. Add gateways, peerings, and relevant next-hop behavior. Route links around subnet titles and keep address labels inside unobstructed header areas.\n\nCheck: Address ranges do not overlap unintentionally. Forward and return paths have compatible routing assumptions. Peering is not assumed to be transitive without platform support. Route notes and boundary crossings remain separate from workload text.\n\nPitfalls: Treating connectivity as proof that security policy permits traffic. Mixing physical and overlay links without a legend.\n\nExample: A hub network connects two spoke networks and an on-premises gateway, with the intended next-hop routes labeled.\n\nAlternatives: Network segmentation map, Physical network diagram."
